--------------------------------------------------------------------------
Why GPLv2, and what is actually MIT
--------------------------------------------------------------------------

Determined by source audit on 2026-09-20 (see docs/phase-0/pre-phase-0-findings.md
section 3). The brief's section 5.4 had assumed GPL contamination came from
MacSurf's modified libcss. That was wrong in its reasoning, right in its
conclusion, and the real boundary is worth stating precisely:

  Component                          Upstream licence    We use it
  ---------------------------------  ------------------  ---------
  libcss, libdom, libhubbub,         MIT                 yes
  libparserutils, libwapcaplet       (per-lib COPYING)
  QuickJS / macQJS                   MIT                 yes
  netsurf core: content handlers,    GPLv2 + OpenSSL     yes  <-- this is
  layout engine, plotters, desktop   exception                 the GPL source
  curl                               MIT                 yes
  OpenSSL                            Apache-2.0          yes  <-- needs the
                                                               exception above
  freetype                           FTL / GPLv2         yes
  harfbuzz                           MIT                 yes
  libpng / libwebp / libjpeg-turbo   BSD-ish             yes
  libvita2d                          MIT                 yes

The five NetSurf core libraries are individually MIT licensed and would not,
on their own, force anything. The GPLv2 obligation comes from browser/netsurf
itself -- the HTML content handler, the layout engine (layout.c, layout_flex.c,
layout_grid.c) and the plotter interface -- which is GPLv2 and which we cannot
do without.

The OpenSSL linking exception is not optional for us: we link vita-sdk OpenSSL.
It is already granted upstream and is reproduced above so it carries forward.

Practical consequence: VitaBrowser ships as GPLv2. Our fixes to the five core
libraries may still be contributed upstream to NetSurf under MIT, because those
files are MIT at their origin -- but only if such a fix is kept free of any
GPL-derived code. Keep core-library patches in a separate series from
browser/netsurf patches so that boundary stays clean (see brief section 14.4).

Full licence texts: GNU GPL v2 -- https://www.gnu.org/licenses/old-licenses/gpl-2.0.txt
The MIT text as it applies to the vendored core libraries is reproduced in each
library's own COPYING file and must be preserved there verbatim.
